# Object `spec` is a helper of `printf`, the object `string.printf.spec`. It
# reads one specifier of the format, starting at the `%` found by `walk`,
# and tells `walk` how to go on: `next` is the position right after the
# specifier, `counter` is the index of the next sequential argument, and
# `piece` is the text the specifier turns into, which is the argument `arg`
# converted and padded. `idx` is the index of the argument the specifier
# picks, so that `walk` can find `arg` before handing it over. Its receiver
# is the `printf` it works for, so it reads the bytes of the format and the
# count of arguments through it.
#
# `spec` reads that specifier from left to right and names every position it
# stops at: `dollar` is the `$` of a positional argument, `flags` opens
# the specifier itself, `digits` follows the `-` and `0` flags, `dot`
# follows the width digits, and `end` holds the conversion letter. Those
# positions, and everything derived from them, are memoized, because the
# language caches through `!` alone, and they are listed alphabetically
# rather than in reading order, because the linter sorts them by name.
#
# The `%s` conversion refuses an argument whose bytes do not decode as UTF-8.
# `false` dataizes to the single byte `00-`, which is also the valid
# one-character encoding of U+0000, so an object knows its own bytes here
# and not its type: FALSE routed through `%s` is printed as that control
# character instead of being rejected. TRUE dataizes to `FF-`, which no
# text encodes, so it is rejected. `%b` remains the conversion to reach
# for a boolean either way.
#
# A number gets no better answer from `%s`: its bytes are printed as text
# when they decode as UTF-8, and refused when they do not, so the outcome
# depends on the value. `42` prints as `@E` and six NUL characters, while `1`
# terminates. `%d` and `%f` are the conversions to reach for a number.
#
# The `%b` conversion refuses an argument whose bytes are neither `00-` nor
# `FF-`, so a text or a number reaching it terminates instead of being
# printed as `false`. The coin lands the other way up as well: the
# one-character text of U+0000 is accepted as a boolean, since its byte is
# the byte of `false`.
#
# The `%d` and `%f` conversions refuse an argument whose bytes are not eight
# wide, naming the argument and the conversion the way `%b` does. Without that
# guard the bytes were read as a number anyway and whatever met them answered
# for itself: `%d` came back naming the receiver of the comparison it fell
# into (#9036), and `%f` called a text a non-finite number, naming what the
# reading made of the argument instead of the argument (#9037). Eight bytes
# is as far as the check reaches, since the bytes of a number are the bytes
# of any other object of the same length, so a text of eight bytes is still
# written as the number they spell.
#
# The `%d` guard names the number it rejects through `as-scientific` and not
# through `%f`, because every magnitude it rejects is past the range `as-fixed`
# writes, and a reason rendered with `%f` destroyed itself instead of arriving.
#
# The `%f` conversion leaves the `cant-print` fallback of `as-fixed` unbound,
# so a number it refuses, a non-finite one or a magnitude of 2^63 or larger,
# terminates with the reason `as-fixed` gives, instead of that reason standing
# in the text where the number was meant to be. Its tests recover from that
# termination instead of declaring it with `-->`, because a returned text of
# more than one byte fails to dataize as a boolean exactly as a termination
# does, and a `-->` test would pass either way.

+architect yegor256@gmail.com
+home https://github.com/objectionary/eo
+package string.printf
+spdx SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) 2016-2026 Objectionary.com
